word on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## CONVERTED OKF STEPS JSON:\n[\n  {\n    \"step_number\": 1,\n    \"sop_step_description\": \"Search for the user by employee ID and confirm lock status.\",\n    \"step_type\": \"tool_call\",\n    \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1: The raw SOP describes searching for a user by employee ID, which matches the `search_user` function. Gate 2: The Solandra platform is active and registered. Gate 3: The function solves the exact problem of locating a user and confirming lock status. Gate 4: The required input `employee_id` is explicitly mentioned in the SOP. Thus, a tool_call is chosen.\",\n    \"platform\": \"solandra\",\n    \"function_name\": \"search_user\",\n    \"comm_inputs\": {\n      \"employee_id\": \"{{{{entities.employee_id}}}}\"\n    },\n    \"command\": null,\n    \"fallback_command\": null,\n    \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n    \"parse_instruction\": null,\n    \"execute_if\": null,\n    \"timeout_seconds\": 30\n  },\n  {\n    \"step_number\": 2,\n    \"sop_step_description\": \"Verify caller identity using at least 2 of 4 items (employee ID, manager name, department, phone last 4).\",\n    \"step_type\": \"tool_call\",\n    \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1: The raw SOP describes caller identity verification, which matches the `verify_caller_identity` function. Gate 2: The Solandra platform is active and registered. Gate 3: The function solves the exact problem of verifying caller identity. Gate 4: The required inputs (employee_id, manager_name, department) are explicitly mentioned in the SOP. `phone_last4` is inferred as a potential verification item. Thus, a tool_call is chosen.\",\n    \"platform\": \"solandra\",\n    \"function_name\": \"verify_caller_identity\",\n    \"comm_inputs\": {\n      \"user_id\": \"{{{{variables.user_id}}}}\",\n      \"employee_id\": \"{{{{entities.employee_id}}}}\",\n      \"manager_name\": \"{{{{entities.manager_name}}}}\",\n      \"department\": \"{{{{entities.department}}}}\",\n      \"phone_last4\": \"{{{{entities.phone_last4}}}}\"\n    },\n    \"command\": null,\n    \"fallback_command\": null,\n    \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n    \"parse_instruction\": null,\n    \"execute_if\": \"variables.user_locked == 'true'\",\n    \"timeout_seconds\": 30\n  },\n  {\n    \"step_number\": 3,\n    \"sop_step_description\": \"Generate a temporary password for the user with force change at next sign-in enabled.\",\n    \"step_type\": \"tool_call\",\n    \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1: The raw SOP describes generating a temporary password, which matches the `generate_temporary_password` function. Gate 2: The Solandra platform is active and registered. Gate 3: The function solves the exact problem of generating a temporary password. Gate 4: The required input `user_id` is available from prior steps. Thus, a tool_call is chosen.\",\n    \"platform\": \"solandra\",\n    \"function_name\": \"generate_temporary_password\",\n    \"comm_inputs\": {\n      \"user_id\": \"{{{{variables.user_id}}}}\"\n    },\n    \"command\": null,\n    \"fallback_command\": null,\n    \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n    \"parse_instruction\": null,\n    \"execute_if\": \"variables.identity_verified == 'true'\",\n    \"timeout_seconds\": 30\n  },\n  {\n    \"step_number\": 4,\n    \"sop_step_description\": \"Resolve the ticket with an audit note detailing the actions taken.\",\n    \"step_type\": \"tool_call\",\n    \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1: The raw SOP describes resolving the ticket with an audit note, which matches the `resolve_ticket` function. Gate 2: The Solandra platform is active and registered. Gate 3: The function solves the exact problem of logging the resolution. Gate 4: The required inputs `ticket_id` and `resolution_note` are explicitly mentioned or inferred from the SOP. Thus, a tool_call is chosen.\",\n    \"platform\": \"solandra\",\n    \"function_name\": \"resolve_ticket\",\n    \"comm_inputs\": {\n      \"ticket_id\": \"{{{{entities.ticket_id}}}}\",\n      \"resolution_note\": \"Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\"\n    },\n    \"command\": null,\n    \"fallback_command\": null,\n    \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n    \"parse_instruction\": null,\n    \"execute_if\": \"variables.temporary_password != 'null'\",\n    \"timeout_seconds\": 30\n  }\n]\n\n## MANDATORY CoT INSPECTION CHECKS:\nBefore outputting the final verdict, complete these 3 inspection reasoning checks in order:\n\n- **Check 1 (100% Action & Tool Recall & Preservation)**: Verify that EVERY SINGLE automated procedure step described in the raw human text (whether a shell CLI command like `ipconfig`/`chkdsk` OR an API tool action like `unlock_user_account`/`admin_reset_password`) is preserved in the OKF steps. If any automated action or tool call is missing from the OKF steps, you MUST mark `is_faithful = false` and add it to `dropped_commands`.\n  *(HUMAN CONFIRMATION EXEMPTION: Human/verbal/ticket confirmations, user clarifications, and manual admin approval steps are currently PAUSED. DO NOT flag omitted verbal/ticket/user confirmation steps as dropped commands.)*\n  *(GUI SETUP EXEMPTION: GUI setup steps such as \"Open Command Prompt\", \"Press Windows Key\", \"Run as administrator\", \"Open Web Browser\", or opening UI windows are intentionally omitted from executable playbooks. DO NOT flag GUI setup steps or UI window launches as dropped commands.)*\n  *(TOOL PARAMETER MAPPING EXEMPTION: When an automated search or query step maps to a registered API tool whose interface requires a specific input key (such as `search_users` requiring `email`), passing the query entity like `{{entities.email}}` or `{{entities.partial_detail}}` in `comm_inputs` satisfies the step. Do NOT flag other raw search criteria like name or employee ID as dropped commands or parameter inaccuracies when mapping to a catalog tool's defined input parameters.)*\n- **Check 2 (Hallucination Prevention)**: Verify that NO extra CLI commands or fabricated API tool calls were created that were NOT in the raw human SOP text.\n- **Check 3 (Fallback & Parameter Accuracy)**: Verify that fallback commands (`fallback_command`), cross-SOP referrals (`fallback_sop_id`), and tool inputs match the raw text's instructions within the constraints of the registered API tools.\n- **Check 4 (4 Verification Gates & Command Executability Audit)**:\n  * For EVERY `tool_call` step: Verify it satisfies the 4 Gates (Gate 1: matches declared platform function, Gate 2: valid platform, Gate 3: problem aligned, Gate 4: required input parameters present in `comm_inputs`).\n  * For EVERY `command` step: Verify that `command` is an actual executable CLI string and is NEVER null or empty.\n  * Verify that purely manual/verbal/phone relay steps were NOT emitted as empty command steps.\n- **Check 5 (Validation Confidence Scoring)**:\n  * `confidence_score`: Decimal between 0.0 and 1.0 evaluating your confidence in this faithfulness inspection (e.g. 1.0 for completely clear SOP comparison, 0.80 if minor ambiguities, 0.50 if highly ambiguous).\n  * `confidence_level`: \"High\" (>= 0.85), \"Medium\" (0.60 - 0.84), or \"Low\" (< 0.60).\n  * `confidence_reason`: Brief rationale for the validation confidence score.\n\n## VERDICT CONTRACT:\n- If all actions are preserved, zero hallucinated commands/tools exist, and all steps are 100% executable, set `is_faithful = true` 